%I A135454 #9 Oct 19 2026 23:44:06
%S A135454 1,6,21,52,103,174,262,360,461,561,660,759,858,957,1056,1155,1254,
%T A135454 1353,1452,1551,1650,1749,1848,1947,2046,2145,2244,2343,2442,2541,
%U A135454 2640,2739,2838,2937,3036,3135,3234,3333,3432,3531,3630,3729,3828
%N A135454 Number of sums payable using exactly n banknotes of denominations 1, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100.
%C A135454 Proof of the formula: subtracting 1 from each banknote, a(n) is the size of the n-fold sumset n*A with A = {0, 4, 9, 19, 49, 99}. By the structure theorem for sumsets (Nathanson; Wu, Chen and Chen), n*A = C U [c, 99*n - d] U (99*n - D) with fixed c, d, C, D for all n >= 9 + 19 + 49 + 99 - 5 = 171, so a(n+1) - a(n) = 99 for n >= 171. A direct computation for 9 <= n <= 3000 completes the proof. - _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 14 2026
%D A135454 M. B. Nathanson, Sums of finite sets of integers, Amer. Math. Monthly 79 (1972), 1010-1012.
%D A135454 J.-D. Wu, F.-J. Chen and Y.-C. Chen, On the structure of the sumsets, Discrete Math. 311 (2011), 408-412.
%H A135454 Pierre Colmant, <a href="/A135454/b135454.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%F A135454 a(n) = 99*n - 330 for n >= 9. - _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 14 2026
%e A135454 n=1: 6 payable sums are 1,5,10,20,50,100;
%e A135454 n=3: the 52 sums are in A135137.
%o A135454 (PARI) a(n) = my(S=[0], D=[1, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100]); for(i=1, n, S=Set(concat(vector(6, j, apply(x->x+D[j], S))))); #S; \\ _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 14 2026
